    "title": "Peptide separation protocols",
    "excerpt":"Trypsin Digestion and TMT Labeling (from Ref 4)   Isobaric labeling with stable isotopes is one of the preferred quantification strategies. Now with the addition of the TMT 10-plex, up to ten samples can be analyzed in a single run, saving you instrument time/money.  Reagents and protocol are provided in the TMTsixplex Isobaric Mass Tagging Kit. Carry out all steps from this point forward using low retention eppendorf tubes.   Materials      Low retention eppendorf tubes.   Dissolution buffer: 100 mM triethyl ammonium bicarbonate (TEAB) (pH 8.0), 0.5 % (w/v) SDS. Aliquot and store at -20 ^o^C.   Reducing solution: 200 mM tris(2-carboxyethyl) phosphine in 200 mM triethyl ammonium bicarbonate (pH 8.0). Prepare fresh.   Alkylation solution: 375 mM iodoacetamide in 100 mM TEAB (pH 8.0). Prepare fresh.   Trypsin solution: 500 ng/μL sequencing-grade modified trypsin (Promega) in 5 mM acetic acid. Store at -20 ^o^C.   SpeedVac.   Acetonitrile (ACN): 100 % HPLC grade.   TMTsixplex Isobaric Mass Tagging Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific).   Quenching solution: 5 % hydroxylamine in 200 mM TEAB (pH 8.0).   Method      Dry down 100 μg of protein from each of the fractions in a SpeedVac and resuspend the protein pellets in 100 μL of dissolution buffer (100 mM TEAB) (pH 8.0), 0.5 % (w/v) SDS.   Add 5 μL of reducing solution (9.5 mM final concentration of tris(2-carboxyethyl) phosphine) and incubate for 1 h at 55 ^o^C.   Add 5 μL of freshly prepared alkylation solution (17 mM final concentration of iodoacetamide) and incubate for 30 min at room temperature in the dark.   Add 5 μL of trypsin solution (2.5 μg/100 μg protein) and incubate overnight at 37 ^o^C.   Reconstitute a tube of each TMT label in 41 μL of 100 % ACN and carefully add to the appropriate sample tube.   Allow labeling reactions to proceed for 1 h at room temperature.   Quench reactions by adding 8 μL of quenching solution and incubate for 15 min at room temperature.   Combine samples at equal amounts and dry down in a SpeedVac.   Either proceed to a C18 cleanup step to remove excess TMT and SDS or some peptide separation protocol like SCX or high pH reversed phase chromatography, to separate the peptides in addition to the cleanup.   Strong Cation Exchange Chromatography (from Ref 4)   SCX is used here to remove excess TMT reagents and SDS and to fractionate the TMT-labeled plasma peptides.   Materials      low retention eppendorf tubes.   Acetonitrile (ACN): 100 % HPLC grade.   Trifl uoroacetic acid (TFA): 100 % HPLC grade.   Buffer A: 50 % ACN, 0.1 % TFA made with HPLC grade water.   Buffer B: 25 % ACN, 0.1 % TFA made with HPLC grade water.   Buffer C: Individual solutions of 100, 200, and 400 mM ammonium acetate, each in 25 % ACN, made with HPLC grade water.   Disposable plastic columns (Thermo Scientific).   SP Sepharose Fast Flow slurry (Sigma-Aldrich).   Method      Prepare cartridges using disposable plastic columns filled with 650 μL of SP Sepharose Fast Flow slurry and a frit placed on top of the resin bed.   Prior to peptide loading, wash cartridges with Buffer B (3 x 1 mL).   Load samples in 3 mL Buffer A. Collect unbound material for later analysis ( see Note 10 ).   Wash bound peptides with 4 mL of Buffer B (4 x 1 mL).   Elute peptides from the cartridge with 2 mL of Buffer C; ammonium acetate at 100, 200, and 400 mM.   Lyophilize the samples to dryness in a vacuum centrifuge.   To ensure the removal of the volatile salt used for SCX, resuspend the samples in Buffer B and re-lyophilize to dryness.   Store samples at -80 ^o^C until further analysis.   Title (from Ref n)   Materials         Method         References      Identification and quantification of N-linked glycoproteins using hydrazide chemistry, stable isotope labeling and mass spectrometry. Zhang H, Li XJ, Martin DB, Aebersold R.Nat Biotechnol. 2003 Jun;21(6):660-6. link   Dopaminergic modulation of the hippocampal neuropil proteome identified by bio-orthogonal non-canonical amino-acid tagging (BONCAT). J.J. Hodas et al., Proteomics 12, 2464-2476 (2012). link   Proteomics of human plasma: A critical comparison of analytical workflows in terms of effort, throughput and outcome. Loic Dayon, Martin Kussmann EuPA Open Proteomics Volume 1, 2013, Pages 8-16 12, 2464-2476 (2012). link   Shotgun Proteomics; Methods and Protocols Editors: Daniel Martins-de-Souza link  ","url": "https://proteomicsresource.washington.edu/methods/protocols04/peptideseparation/"
