    "title": "UWPR Pressure Cell",
    "excerpt":"   Pressure cells are designed to pack/load microbore capillaries, such as polyimide coated fused silica. They can be used to pack columns and pre-columns (traps) with a slurry of beads or to load samples onto packed traps or columns. We routinely use 360 µm OD capillaries with 50-150 µm ID’s to pack columns and traps with 3-5 µm beads of various chemistries (C18, SCX).   There are a couple of good movies at benchfly.com that show how it’s done:      Video: How to pull a capillary tip for LC/MS   Video: How to pack a capillary column   Video: How to Bomb-load Capillary Tips or Frits for LC/MS   The documents on this site are intended to help you setup your own packing station and pack/load columns. You can order the machined parts from emachineshop.com (or any other machine shop) or buy a pressure cell from one of the commercial sources (some of them are listed in the documents).      Packing_Capillary_Columns.pdf        LC-MS commonly used consumables (xls)       UWPR_Pressure_Cell_for_packing_capillary_columns.pdf   UWPR_Pressure_Cell_Material_List.xls      The files below are CAD drawings for the pressure cell body and lid.  Download the free CAD drawing software from http://www.emachineshop.com to view the files:      UWPR_PressureCellLid.ems   UWPR_PressureCellBody.ems   Here are some links to a commercially available pressure cells:   ESI Source Solutions sells a lot of useful LC-MS consumables, including the Dr.Maisch bulk packing materials.   Next Advance offers several Capillary column packing kits.  they also offer a kit to make your own frits the Frit Kit (also available from FisherSci) and a full line of accessories to setup and maintain the pressure cell.   Scientific Instrument Services or SIS is a distributer of the Next Advance Pressure Cell  as well as the Frit Kit   As an alternative to packing your own columns there are various commercially available options out there. Note some columns will require a separate spray tip.  ESI Source Solutions sells columns from ionopticks, Bruker, PremierLCMS etc  ","url": "https://proteomicsresource.washington.edu/methods/protocols05/pressurecell/"
